  "slug": "optimal-transport-flow-matching-ea35",
  "summary": "Binding and function predictors increasingly report generalization under stricter evaluation, with dataset properties often outweighing encoder choice.",
  "synthesis": "Most items apply multimodal or structure-aware learners to binding and function prediction, but the shared thread is how generalization is tested, not the architectures themselves. The OneProt profiling study finds dataset properties dominate performance over encoder choice for allosteric versus orthosteric site classification. The M2OR contrastive learning preprint targets generalization on olfactory receptor binding across M2OR and iOBPdb. SeekRBP treats negative sampling as a multi-armed bandit problem, making negative construction an explicit modeling component. FlexENN adds a new evaluation set spanning folded and disordered-partner complexes, and the RNA-small molecule survey covers evaluation alongside methods. Together these treat split design, negatives and dataset composition as central to interpreting reported performance.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
